# Sheriff/Lawman

**Alignments.** Any, but typically good or unprincipled. Most are honourable,
courageous and resourceful, and live by the Code of the New West.

The town sheriff of the New West is the embodiment of the law. He does the
everyday work of a police officer and a detective and, often, carries
responsibility for the wellbeing of the whole town besides. He is the voice of
reason on questions of justice, and an adviser, confidant and friend. In the
smallest places he splits his time between several communities, farms and
ranches spread across a wide area, and may act as judge, jury and executioner.

He and his deputies break up brawls and head off showdowns, settle disputes over
property and accusations of cheating, handle drunks and scalawags, investigate
robberies and murders, and try to bring criminals to justice. The presence of a
sheriff is itself a signal to bandits and troublemakers that a town will not
put up with them.

## Scarcity

Lawmen are a luxury in most small towns. Only about two in five towns of a
hundred to six hundred people have a resident sheriff, and even three in ten
large towns of seven hundred to three thousand have neither sheriff nor
champion. Some places are covered by a circuit - a sheriff or a body like Reid''s
Rangers, the Justice Rangers or the Cyber-Knights riding a wide territory and
visiting weekly, monthly or every few months.

That means the lawman may be days away from trouble, and may have to stay
somewhere for weeks to settle something serious, leaving the rest of his circuit
to fend for itself. It is what makes deputies valuable, though a deputy is
usually less experienced and less charismatic and is sometimes simply
overwhelmed.

## The job

Anyone with a sense of justice, a willingness to stand, and the ability to
handle himself can pin on a badge; gunfighters, gunslingers, cowboys,
mercenaries, Juicers, sorcerers and adventurers all have. But there is a breed
whose sense of justice runs strongly enough that they give their lives to
protecting the innocent and bringing order out of anarchy, and this class is
written for those.

Sometimes the lawman stands against the very people he swore to protect, when a
lynch mob, vigilantes or a Justice Ranger want blood without a trial. He is
often at odds with bounty hunters, gunslingers, gunfighters and powerful,
sometimes corrupt, ranchers and business owners. It is a difficult life and can
be a very short one.

## Lore

Also known as the Lawman, the Law Dog, the Tin Star and the Marshall. Before the
Rifts, *Marshall* meant a federal officer appointed by a governor or a federal
agency; in the New West it is just another word for a sheriff. His sidekick is
his deputy, though lawmen also ride with other sheriffs, gunfighters and
trustworthy adventurers - and the book suggests the Saloon Bum and the Barmaid
as another pair of likely sidekicks or informers.
